# Pipeline Design Documentation
## Digital Mental Health NLP Pipeline for Asian Adolescents


## 1. Design Principles

| Principle | Description |
|---|---|
| **Clinical safety first** | No automated action without human review |
| **Interpretability** | Every output includes SHAP feature weights |
| **Cultural validity** | Models validated on Asian adolescent populations |
| **Multilingual parity** | Mandarin and English treated with equal rigour |
| **Minimal footprint** | Lightweight models for resource-constrained settings |
| **Privacy by design** | No persistent storage of raw text |
| **Workflow integration** | System adapts to counsellors — not the other way round (Chee, 2026) |

---

## 2. Product-Market Fit

| User | Jobs-to-be-done | Pains Relieved | Gains Created |
|---|---|---|---|
| **School Counsellor** | Identify at-risk students early | Reactive crisis workload | Proactive alerts + SHAP explanations |
| **REACH/Clinical Officer** | Prioritise cases efficiently | Information overload | Risk-ranked case list with trajectory |
| **School Principal** | Demonstrate duty of care | Liability concerns | Documented screening + audit trail |
| **Adolescent (opt-in)** | Get help before crisis | Stigma of self-referral | Anonymous early support pathway |
| **Researcher** | Study adolescent mental health | No longitudinal data | Anonymised population trends |

**Value Proposition:** For school counsellors and mental health teams who struggle to identify at-risk adolescents before crisis — this pipeline provides early, interpretable, multilingual risk detection that fits existing workflows, reducing reactive caseloads and enabling proactive care.

---

## 3. System Architecture

**Tier 1 — Screening:**
Chinese MentalBERT (Ji et al., 2024) classifies input text as distress / no distress. Languages: Mandarin (Traditional + Simplified) + English. Benchmark: F1 = 88.39% on SOS-1K Chinese suicide risk dataset. Tuned for high sensitivity. Low distress exits pipeline; high distress proceeds to Tier 2.

**Tier 2 — Risk Scoring:**
XGBoost + SHAP generates risk score 0–100 with interpretable feature weights across 24 features (linguistic, temporal, social, cultural, Tier 1 outputs). Every score includes a clinician-readable feature breakdown.

**Tier 3 — Temporal Forecasting:**
LSTM with 30-day lookback generates 7-day risk trajectory. Grounded in Eckstein et al. (2026): human behaviour encodes risk history at multiple timescales. Output: daily risk probability + recommended intervention timing.

**Output / Action Layer:**
- Low (0–30) → Digital self-care platform
- Medium (31–65) → Trained counsellor referral + peer support
- High (66–100) → School-based escalation + emergency services

> ALL high-risk outputs require human clinical review. No automated action at any tier.

