## 4. Feature Engineering

### 4.1 Linguistic Features (8)

| Feature | Clinical Basis |
|---|---|
| Hopelessness count | Strongest predictor of suicide risk (Beck, 1974) |
| Isolation count | Key depression symptom; predicts escalation |
| Somatic complaint count | Culturally normative distress proxy in Asian populations |
| Academic stress count | Exam periods correlate with crisis presentations |
| Positive language count (inverse) | Low positive affect predicts depression onset |
| Help-seeking signal count | Explicit and implicit support requests |
| Negation density | Clinical meaning changes with negation |
| Intensifier density | Emotional intensity markers |

### 4.2 Temporal Features (5)

| Feature | Clinical Basis |
|---|---|
| Late-night posting (11pm–4am) | Disrupted sleep is both symptom and risk factor |
| Early morning posting (5–7am) | Sleep disruption indicator |
| Business hours posting | Low daytime activity associated with depression |
| Hour sine encoding | Cyclical encoding preserves time relationships |
| Hour cosine encoding | Cyclical encoding complement |

### 4.3 Cultural Context Features (4)

| Feature | Clinical Basis |
|---|---|
| Code-switching detected | Higher emotional arousal in bilingual speakers |
| Singlish/local markers | APAC-specific distress expression |
| Distress emoji/sentiment tags | Emoji carry emotional signal in adolescent text |
| Text length normalised | Very short posts may indicate withdrawal |

